##########################################################################

uthorization comes after authentication:

"This is Shah"
      ↓
"What is Shah allowed to do?"
      ↓
Allowed / Not allowed

For example, imagine your application has two types of users:

admin
user

You might have:

Endpoint	user	admin
GET /profile	✅	✅
GET /orders	✅	✅
POST /orders	✅	✅
DELETE /users/{id}	❌	✅
GET /admin/dashboard	❌	✅

So the flow becomes:

                 Request
                    ↓
              JWT verification
                    ↓
              Who is the user?
                    ↓
                 "shah"
                    ↓
              What is their role?
                    ↓
                  "admin"
                    ↓
             Is admin allowed?
               ↙          ↘
             YES           NO
              ↓             ↓
          Execute API      403

A valid JWT does not automatically mean the user can do everything.

For example:

JWT valid ✅
User authenticated ✅
Role = user
Trying to access /admin
                    ↓
Authorization fails ❌
                    ↓
403 Forbidden

This is why we distinguish:

401 Unauthorized
→ Authentication problem
→ "I don't know/trust who you are."

403 Forbidden
→ Authorization problem
→ "I know who you are, but you're not allowed to do this."

We already have a User table.

Let's add:

id
name
age
username
password_hash
role

For example:

1 | Shah | 26 | shah | $argon2... | admin
2 | Ali  | 25 | ali  | $argon2... | user

The role therefore belongs to the user's database record.

For our learning implementation, we'll also put the role into the JWT:

{
    "sub": "shah",
    "role": "admin",
    "exp": "..."
}

Then the protected API can determine:

JWT
 ↓
sub = shah
role = admin
 ↓
Authorization decision

Dependency composition : one dependency can depend on another dependency

Right now an admin endpoint looks like this:

Request
   ↓
get_current_user()
   ↓
Authenticated user
   ↓
Check role == admin
   ↓
Endpoint

But if you have many admin endpoints:

DELETE /users/{id}
GET    /admin/dashboard
POST   /admin/products
DELETE /admin/orders/{id}

you don't want to repeat:

if current_user.role != "admin":
    raise HTTPException(...)

everywhere.

Instead, we'll create another dependency:

get_current_user()
       ↓
   current_user
       ↓
 require_admin()
       ↓
   admin check
       ↓
   endpoint


################################# ERRORS ##################################################################

Backend ERRORS : Expected erros (4XX , 5XX)

Intentioal Erros: Unexpected erros  (CAUSES Internal server error)

Expected API problem
        ↓
HTTPException
        ↓
Controlled HTTP response

Unexpected programming/system problem (ex. ZeroDivisionError)
        ↓
Python Exception
        ↓
500 Internal Server Error

global exception handling?

Imagine you have 50 endpoints.

You don't want every endpoint to contain:

try:
    ...
except SomeException:
    ...

Instead, FastAPI allows you to define a handler that says:

"Whenever this particular exception occurs anywhere in my application, handle it this way."

Conceptually:

                 Any endpoint
                     │
                     ▼
              Exception occurs
                     │
                     ▼
          Global exception handler
                     │
                     ▼
              Standard response

This becomes particularly useful for unexpected errors.

############################################# MiddleWare ############################################################

Middleware inserts an additional processing layer around that flow:

Client
   │
   │ Request
   ▼
┌─────────────────┐
│   Middleware    │
│   BEFORE        │
└────────┬────────┘
         │
         ▼
   FastAPI Route
         │
         ▼
   Business Logic
         │
         ▼
┌─────────────────┐
│   Middleware    │
│   AFTER         │
└────────┬────────┘
         │
         ▼
      Response
         │
         ▼
       Client

The important idea is:

Middleware can execute code before the request reaches your endpoint and after your endpoint finishes.

                 CLIENT
                     │
                     ▼
              HTTP REQUEST
                     │
                     ▼
          ┌────────────────────┐
          │     MIDDLEWARE     │
          │                    │
          │  BEFORE processing │
          └─────────┬──────────┘
                    │
                    ▼
              FastAPI Router
                    │
                    ▼
                Endpoint
                    │
             ┌──────┴──────┐
             │             │
          Database       API call
             │             │
             └──────┬──────┘
                    │
                    ▼
                 Response
                    │
                    ▼
          ┌────────────────────┐
          │     MIDDLEWARE     │
          │                    │
          │  AFTER processing  │
          └─────────┬──────────┘
                    │
                    ▼
                  CLIENT

And when an operation involves asynchronous I/O:

Endpoint
   ↓
await database/API operation
   ↓
This task pauses
   ↓
Event loop handles other work
   ↓
I/O completes
   ↓
This task resumes
The three things to remember

async def

This function can operate asynchronously and use await.

await

Pause this coroutine while an asynchronous operation completes, allowing other async work to progress.

Middleware

Code that wraps HTTP request processing and can run before and after your endpoint.

